</math> is the radius of a base of the cylinder and <math alttext="b"><mi>b</mi>
</math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that the cylinder shown has a radius of <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math> and a height of <math alttext="h"><mi>h</mi>
</math>. It follows that the volume of the cylinder shown is equal to <math alttext="pi r squared h"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the second right circular cylinder has a radius of <math alttext="upper R"><mi>R</mi>
</math> and a height of <math alttext="upper H"><mi>H</mi>
</math>. It follows that the volume of the second cylinder is equal to <math alttext="pi upper R squared upper H"><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>H</mi></math>. Choice C gives <math alttext="upper R equals 7 r"><mrow>
	<mi>R</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>7</mn>
		<mi>r</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="upper H equals 8 h"><mrow>
	<mi>H</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mrow>
		<mn>8</mn>
		<mi>h</mi>
	</mrow>
</mrow>
</math>. Substituting <math alttext="7 r"><mrow>
