</math>. The measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by <math alttext="StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the given angle measure, <math alttext="StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></math>, by&nbsp;<math alttext="StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext="left parenthesis StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians right parenthesis left parenthesis StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi r a d i a n s EndFraction right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>radians</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math>, which simplifies to <math alttext="192"><mn>192</mn>
</math> degrees.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a0cacec1","external_id":"889ad56f-33a9-4c3b-b4d8-24aba4870171","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"1e911e0b-68f0-45d9-b12b-f23ee21a1b9b","uId":"8a8c3323-e553-4b4a-b745-fec240211191","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>An angle has a measure of <math alttext=\"StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>16</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>15</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians. What is the measure of the angle, in <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">degrees</span>?</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["192"],"correct_answer":["192"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"192\"><mn>192</mn>\n</math>. The measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by <math alttext=\"StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the given angle measure, <math alttext=\"StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></math>, by&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"left parenthesis StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians right parenthesis left parenthesis StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi r a d i a n s EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>radians</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math>, which simplifies to <math alttext=\"192\"><mn>192</mn>\n</math> degrees.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959841,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"a0cacec1","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1691007959841,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"889ad56f-33a9-4c3b-b4d8-24aba4870171","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"8a8c3323-e553-4b4a-b745-fec240211191","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["192"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"192\"><mn>192</mn>\n</math>. The measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by <math alttext=\"StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying the given angle measure, <math alttext=\"StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></math>, by&nbsp;<math alttext=\"StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi radians EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"left parenthesis StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction radians right parenthesis left parenthesis StartFraction 180 degrees Over pi r a d i a n s EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mtext>radians</mtext></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>degrees</mtext></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>radians</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math>, which simplifies to <math alttext=\"192\"><mn>192</mn>\n</math> degrees.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p>An angle has a measure of <math alttext=\"StartFraction 16 pi Over 15 EndFraction\"><mrow>\n\t<mfrac>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>16</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>&pi;</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mn>15</mn>\n\t</mfrac>\n</mrow>\n</math> radians. What is the measure of the angle, in <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">degrees</span>?</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","externalid":"889ad56f-33a9-4c3b-b4d8-24aba4870171","templateid":"20ccb8dc-2f2b-4161-8250-1a60e6c26cfa","vaultid":"1e911e0b-68f0-45d9-b12b-f23ee21a1b9b","type":"spr","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["192"]},"createDate":1691007959841,"updateDate":1691007959841}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959841, 1691007959841),
    ($SATQ$a2659088$SATQ$, $SATQ$0baecf54-d44d-4084-ba8f-a8fb9f7596bf$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A right circular cylinder has a height of <math alttext="8 meters left parenthesis m right parenthesis"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mtext>m</mtext></mfenced></math> and a base with a radius of <math alttext="12 m"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>m</mtext></math>. What is the volume, <math alttext="in m cubed"><mtext>in</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, of the cylinder?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3a0ca88e-99b1-487a-bbf6-d34db01ceac6","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8 pi\"><mn>8</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dab3c904-3eeb-4fdf-ba49-6ae58c7badcb","content":"<p><math alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"226af872-f11b-4f23-a154-e4f87788894e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"768 pi\"><mn>768</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e1e53ade-856e-462b-a8c5-3a32960a2007","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1,152 pi\"><mn>1,152</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e1e53ade-856e-462b-a8c5-3a32960a2007"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is correct. The volume, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V"><mi>V</mi></math>, of a right circular cylinder is given by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> is the radius of the circular base and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="h"><mi>h</mi></math> is the height of the cylinder. It&rsquo;s given that the cylinder has a height of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="8"><mn>8</mn></math> meters and a base with a radius of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="12"><mn>12</mn></math> meters. Substituting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="12"><mn>12</mn></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="8"><mn>8</mn></math> for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="h"><mi>h</mi></math> in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi r squared h"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals pi left parenthesis 12 right parenthesis squared left parenthesis 8 right parenthesis"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced><mn>8</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper V equals 1,152 pi"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1,152</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>. Therefore, the volume, in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="m Superscript 3"><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, of the cylinder is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1,152 pi"><mn>1,152</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the volume, in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="m Superscript 3"><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, of a cylinder with a radius of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="8"><mn>8</mn></math> meters and a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="12"><mn>12</mn></math> meters.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a2659088","external_id":"0baecf54-d44d-4084-ba8f-a8fb9f7596bf","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"3efb3d9a-651f-4fbf-ae06-0969d5f8d6ec","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A right circular cylinder has a height of <math alttext=\"8 meters left parenthesis m right parenthesis\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mtext>m</mtext></mfenced></math> and a base with a radius of <math alttext=\"12 m\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>m</mtext></math>. What is the volume, <math alttext=\"in m cubed\"><mtext>in</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, of the cylinder?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3a0ca88e-99b1-487a-bbf6-d34db01ceac6","content":"<p><math alttext=\"8 pi\"><mn>8</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dab3c904-3eeb-4fdf-ba49-6ae58c7badcb","content":"<p><math alttext=\"20 pi\"><mn>20</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"226af872-f11b-4f23-a154-e4f87788894e","content":"<p><math alttext=\"768 pi\"><mn>768</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"e1e53ade-856e-462b-a8c5-3a32960a2007","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1,152 pi\"><mn>1,152</mn>\n<mi>&pi;</mi>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["e1e53ade-856e-462b-a8c5-3a