    ($SATQ$a490003a$SATQ$, $SATQ$ca7c086a-76e8-4959-b23f-94d448a1c1c3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Area and volume$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The width of a rectangle is <math alttext="7"><mn>7</mn>
</math> centimeters. The length of the rectangle is <math alttext="40"><mn>40</mn>
</math> centimeters longer than the width. What is the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0d8ff629-460e-4680-b9a8-132b822ef820","content":"<p><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b770a022-6958-4385-9387-7b03702cec08","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"a0a72ea7-59bf-43e9-bc4b-05ff5494739f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605","content":"<p><math alttext=\"329\"><mn>329</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the width of this rectangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="7"><mn>7</mn></math> centimeters and that the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="40"><mn>40</mn></math> centimeters longer than the width. Therefore, the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="7 plus 40"><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>40</mn></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="47"><mn>47</mn></math>, centimeters. The area of a rectangle can be found by multiplying its length and its width. Therefore the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 47 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mn>47</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="329"><mn>329</mn></math>.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the width, in centimeters, not the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a490003a","external_id":"ca7c086a-76e8-4959-b23f-94d448a1c1c3","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"eebae2a4-fba0-4254-8949-4bba793dd326","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.A.","skill_desc":"Area and volume","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The width of a rectangle is <math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math> centimeters. The length of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"40\"><mn>40</mn>\n</math> centimeters longer than the width. What is the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0d8ff629-460e-4680-b9a8-132b822ef820","content":"<p><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b770a022-6958-4385-9387-7b03702cec08","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"a0a72ea7-59bf-43e9-bc4b-05ff5494739f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605","content":"<p><math alttext=\"329\"><mn>329</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the width of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn></math> centimeters and that the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"40\"><mn>40</mn></math> centimeters longer than the width. Therefore, the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"7 plus 40\"><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>40</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"47\"><mn>47</mn></math>, centimeters. The area of a rectangle can be found by multiplying its length and its width. Therefore the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 47 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mn>47</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"329\"><mn>329</mn></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the width, in centimeters, not the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017724,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"a490003a","skill_cd":"S.A.","score_band_range_cd":3,"skill_desc":"Area and volume","createDate":1755117017724,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"ca7c086a-76e8-4959-b23f-94d448a1c1c3","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"eebae2a4-fba0-4254-8949-4bba793dd326","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The width of a rectangle is <math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math> centimeters. The length of the rectangle is <math alttext=\"40\"><mn>40</mn>\n</math> centimeters longer than the width. What is the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle?</p>","keys":["31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"0d8ff629-460e-4680-b9a8-132b822ef820","content":"<p><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b770a022-6958-4385-9387-7b03702cec08","content":"<p><math alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"a0a72ea7-59bf-43e9-bc4b-05ff5494739f","content":"<p><math alttext=\"54\"><mn>54</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"31506b75-3a31-43e3-ac9e-c862fd746605","content":"<p><math alttext=\"329\"><mn>329</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the width of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn></math> centimeters and that the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"40\"><mn>40</mn></math> centimeters longer than the width. Therefore, the length of this rectangle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"7 plus 40\"><mn>7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>40</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"47\"><mn>47</mn></math>, centimeters. The area of a rectangle can be found by multiplying its length and its width. Therefore the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis left parenthesis 47 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mn>47</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"329\"><mn>329</mn></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the width, in centimeters, not the area, in square centimeters, of this rectangle.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","externalid":"ca7c086a-76e8-4959-b23f-94d448a1c1c3","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1755117017724,"updateDate":1755117017724}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017724, 1755117017724),
    ($SATQ$a4bd60a3$SATQ$, $SATQ$1dd30616-5d7f-4401-8dd7-811d477d90d8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$94f275e5-badf-45c6-819b-398820b0374c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is <math alttext="624"><mn>624</mn>
</math> centimeters. The height of this triangle is <math alttext="k StartRoot 3 EndRoot"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math> centimeters, where <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> is a constant. What is the value of <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["104"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["104"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="104"><mn>104</mn>
</math>. An equilateral triangle is a triangle in which all three sides have the same length and all three angles have a measure of <math alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. The height of the triangle,&nbsp;<math alttext="k StartRoot 3 EndRoot"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, is the length of the altitude from one vertex. The altitude divides the equilateral triangle into two congruent 30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles, where the altitude is the side across from the&nbsp;<math alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle in each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle. Since the altitude has a length of <math alttext="k StartRoot 3 EndRoot"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, it follows from the properties of&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles that the side across from each <math alttext="30 degree"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle has a length of <math alttext="k"><mi>k</mi>
</math> and each hypotenuse has a length of <math alttext="2 k"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>k</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. In this case, the hypotenuse of each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle is a side of the equilateral triangle; therefore, each side length of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext="2 k"><mrow>
	<mn>2</mn>
	<mi>k</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of each side. It's given that the perimeter of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext="624"><mn>624</mn>
</math>; therefore, <math alttext="2 k plus 2 k plus 2 k equals 624"><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>, or <math alttext="6 k equals 624"><mn>6</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="6"><mn>6</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="k equals 104"><mrow>
	<mi>k</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>104</mn>
</mrow>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a4bd60a3","external_id":"1dd30616-5d7f-4401-8dd7-811d477d90d8","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"94f275e5-badf-45c6-819b-398820b0374c","uId":"4e0ef4df-94fb-41e7-b8e9-df0d85a6f1ee","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"624\"><mn>624</mn>\n</math> centimeters. The height of this triangle is <math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math> centimeters, where <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is a constant. What is the value of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["104"],"correct_answer":["104"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"104\"><mn>104</mn>\n</math>. An equilateral triangle is a triangle in which all three sides have the same length and all three angles have a measure of <math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. The height of the triangle,&nbsp;<math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, is the length of the altitude from one vertex. The altitude divides the equilateral triangle into two congruent 30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles, where the altitude is the side across from the&nbsp;<math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle in each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle. Since the altitude has a length of <math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, it follows from the properties of&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles that the side across from each <math alttext=\"30 degree\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle has a length of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> and each hypotenuse has a length of <math alttext=\"2 k\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. In this case, the hypotenuse of each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle is a side of the equilateral triangle; therefore, each side length of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"2 k\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of each side. It's given that the perimeter of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"624\"><mn>624</mn>\n</math>; therefore, <math alttext=\"2 k plus 2 k plus 2 k equals 624\"><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"6 k equals 624\"><mn>6</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"k equals 104\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>104</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959840,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"a4bd60a3","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1691007959840,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":null,"external_id":"1dd30616-5d7f-4401-8dd7-811d477d90d8","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"4e0ef4df-94fb-41e7-b8e9-df0d85a6f1ee","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["104"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"104\"><mn>104</mn>\n</math>. An equilateral triangle is a triangle in which all three sides have the same length and all three angles have a measure of <math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>. The height of the triangle,&nbsp;<math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, is the length of the altitude from one vertex. The altitude divides the equilateral triangle into two congruent 30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles, where the altitude is the side across from the&nbsp;<math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle in each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle. Since the altitude has a length of <math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>, it follows from the properties of&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangles that the side across from each <math alttext=\"30 degree\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> angle has a length of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> and each hypotenuse has a length of <math alttext=\"2 k\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. In this case, the hypotenuse of each&nbsp;30-60-90&nbsp;right triangle is a side of the equilateral triangle; therefore, each side length of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"2 k\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of each side. It's given that the perimeter of the equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"624\"><mn>624</mn>\n</math>; therefore, <math alttext=\"2 k plus 2 k plus 2 k equals 624\"><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"6 k equals 624\"><mn>6</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>624</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"k equals 104\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>k</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>104</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The perimeter of an equilateral triangle is <math alttext=\"624\"><mn>624</mn>\n</math> centimeters. The height of this triangle is <math alttext=\"k StartRoot 3 EndRoot\"><mi>k</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math> centimeters, where <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math> is a constant. What is the value of <math alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi>\n</math>?</p>","externalid":"1dd30616-5d7f-4401-8dd7-811d477d90d8","templateid":"06233fba-be3b-4f62-bb10-626303e5059e","vaultid":"94f275e5-badf-45c6-819b-398820b0374c","type":"spr","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["104"]},"createDate":1691007959840,"updateDate":1691007959840}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959840, 1691007959840),
    ($SATQ$a4c0547f$SATQ$, $SATQ$2124991b-31a9-4ce3-a963-094142d78f70$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Right triangles and trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">In triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mrow>
	<mi>X</mi>
	<mi>Y</mi>
	<mi>Z</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> is a right angle and the length of <math alttext="line segment upper Y upper Z"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> units. If <math alttext="tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>
	<mn>12</mn>
	<mn>35</mn>
</mfrac>
</math>, what is the perimeter, in units, of triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mrow>
	<mi>X</mi>
	<mi>Y</mi>
	<mi>Z</mi>
</mrow>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fa483ed8-832e-431f-8400-cd6ba21d21f9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"188\"><mn>188</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12","content":"<p><math alttext=\"168\"><mn>168</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"805d0948-7a88-4467-880d-ff1089e33c01","content":"<p><math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"456ed435-01c5-4b0b-a751-ce35b6242242","content":"<p><math alttext=\"71\"><mn>71</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It's given that angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> in triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is a right angle. Thus, side <math alttext="upper Y upper Z"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg opposite angle <math alttext="upper X"><mi>X</mi>
</math> and side <math alttext="upper X upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext="upper X"><mi>X</mi>
</math>. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that <math alttext="tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's given that <math alttext="tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> and the length of side <math alttext="upper Y upper Z"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> units. Substituting <math alttext="StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> for <math alttext="tangent upper X"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi></math> and <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="upper Y upper Z"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext="tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext="StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction equals StartFraction 24 Over upper X upper Z EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext="35 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis"><mn>35</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> yields <math alttext="12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 24 left parenthesis 35 right parenthesis"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mfenced><mn>35</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 840"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>840</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="12"><mn>12</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="upper X upper Z equals 70"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></math>. The length <math alttext="upper X upper Y"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that if a right triangle has legs with lengths of <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="b"><mi>b</mi>
</math> and a hypotenuse with length <math alttext="c"><mi>c</mi>
</math>, then <math alttext="a squared plus b squared equals c squared"><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math alttext="70"><mn>70</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="a"><mi>a</mi>
</math> and <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="b"><mi>b</mi>
</math> in this equation yields <math alttext="70 squared plus 24 squared equals c squared"><msup><mn>70</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>24</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="5,476 equals c squared"><mn>5,476</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the square root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext="plus or minus 74 equals c"><mo>&plusmn;</mo><mn>74</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math>. Since the length of the hypotenuse must be positive, <math alttext="74 equals c"><mrow>
<mn>74</mn>
<mo>=</mo>
<mi>c</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the length of <math alttext="upper X upper Y"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> is <math alttext="74"><mn>74</mn>
</math> units. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of all sides. Thus, <math alttext="left parenthesis 74 plus 70 plus 24 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>74</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> units, or <math alttext="168"><mn>168</mn>
</math> units, is the perimeter of triangle <math alttext="upper X upper Y upper Z"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This would be the perimeter, in units, for a right triangle where the length of side <math alttext="upper Y upper Z"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext="12"><mn>12</mn>
</math> units, not <math alttext="24"><mn>24</mn>
</math> units.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"a4c0547f","external_id":"2124991b-31a9-4ce3-a963-094142d78f70","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"0f4b236a-c59f-42f3-bdb1-1290f4b38312","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.C.","skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is a right angle and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> units. If <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>\n\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t<mn>35</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math>, what is the perimeter, in units, of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fa483ed8-832e-431f-8400-cd6ba21d21f9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"188\"><mn>188</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12","content":"<p><math alttext=\"168\"><mn>168</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"805d0948-7a88-4467-880d-ff1089e33c01","content":"<p><math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"456ed435-01c5-4b0b-a751-ce35b6242242","content":"<p><math alttext=\"71\"><mn>71</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It's given that angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> in triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is a right angle. Thus, side <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg opposite angle <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi>\n</math> and side <math alttext=\"upper X upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi>\n</math>. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's given that <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> and the length of side <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> units. Substituting <math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> for <math alttext=\"tangent upper X\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction equals StartFraction 24 Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"35 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis\"><mn>35</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> yields <math alttext=\"12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 24 left parenthesis 35 right parenthesis\"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mfenced><mn>35</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 840\"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>840</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper X upper Z equals 70\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></math>. The length <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that if a right triangle has legs with lengths of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math> and a hypotenuse with length <math alttext=\"c\"><mi>c</mi>\n</math>, then <math alttext=\"a squared plus b squared equals c squared\"><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"70\"><mn>70</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi>\n</math> in this equation yields <math alttext=\"70 squared plus 24 squared equals c squared\"><msup><mn>70</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>24</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext=\"5,476 equals c squared\"><mn>5,476</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. Taking the square root of both sides of this equation yields <math alttext=\"plus or minus 74 equals c\"><mo>&plusmn;</mo><mn>74</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math>. Since the length of the hypotenuse must be positive, <math alttext=\"74 equals c\"><mrow>\n<mn>74</mn>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mi>c</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the length of <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"74\"><mn>74</mn>\n</math> units. The perimeter of a triangle is the sum of the lengths of all sides. Thus, <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 74 plus 70 plus 24 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>74</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> units, or <math alttext=\"168\"><mn>168</mn>\n</math> units, is the perimeter of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This would be the perimeter, in units, for a right triangle where the length of side <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math> units, not <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> units.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1743430554980,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"a4c0547f","skill_cd":"S.C.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Right triangles and trigonometry","createDate":1743430554980,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"2124991b-31a9-4ce3-a963-094142d78f70","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"0f4b236a-c59f-42f3-bdb1-1290f4b38312","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is a right angle and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper Y upper Z\"><mover><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> units. If <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac>\n\t<mn>12</mn>\n\t<mn>35</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math>, what is the perimeter, in units, of triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>X</mi>\n\t<mi>Y</mi>\n\t<mi>Z</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"fa483ed8-832e-431f-8400-cd6ba21d21f9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"188\"><mn>188</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"654ee186-f3de-49f8-8bd4-842e487f2a12","content":"<p><math alttext=\"168\"><mn>168</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"805d0948-7a88-4467-880d-ff1089e33c01","content":"<p><math alttext=\"84\"><mn>84</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"456ed435-01c5-4b0b-a751-ce35b6242242","content":"<p><math alttext=\"71\"><mn>71</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It's given that angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> in triangle <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is a right angle. Thus, side <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg opposite angle <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi>\n</math> and side <math alttext=\"upper X upper Z\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is the leg adjacent to angle <math alttext=\"upper X\"><mi>X</mi>\n</math>. The tangent of an acute angle in a right triangle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. It's given that <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> and the length of side <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> units. Substituting <math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac></math> for <math alttext=\"tangent upper X\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi></math> and <math alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"upper Y upper Z\"><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></math> in the equation <math alttext=\"tangent upper X equals StartFraction upper Y upper Z Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 12 Over 35 EndFraction equals StartFraction 24 Over upper X upper Z EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>35</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"35 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis\"><mn>35</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> yields <math alttext=\"12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 24 left parenthesis 35 right parenthesis\"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mfenced><mn>35</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"12 left parenthesis upper X upper Z right parenthesis equals 840\"><mn>12</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>840</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext=\"12\"><mn>12</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"upper X upper Z equals 70\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>70</mn></math>. The length <math alttext=\"upper X upper Y\"><mi>X</mi><mi>Y</mi></math> can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that if a right triangle has legs with lengths of <math alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi>\n</math> and 